\pard\qc\sl240\slmult1 \fi0 {Figure {\*\bkmkstart BMF_network}1{\*\bkmkend BMF_network}: Glycerol to Hydrocarbon (GTH) Reaction Network}{\field{\*\fldinst TC "1 Glycerol to Hydrocarbon (GTH) Reaction Network" \\f f}{\fldrslt }}\par
}\pard\qj\sl240\slmult1 \sb360 \fi0 {\s4\ql\sb240\sa60\keepn\f13\b\fs32 4.2  Absence of Mass and Heat Transfer Effects\par
}\pard\qj\sl240\slmult1 \sb60 \fi0 As shown in {\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BMF_transfer \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{?}}}, using the well known procedure, the absence of both internal and external mass transfer limitations were confirmed before conducting kinetic measurements. By only varying packed catalyst particle diameter ({{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ {{\i d}\\s\\do5({\fs16 {\i p}})}}}{\fldrslt }}}) in the range of 100 micron < {{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ {{\i d}\\s\\do5({\fs16 {\i p}})}}}{\fldrslt }}} < 1000 micron, {\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BMF_transfer \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{?}}}(a) was obtained. It shows that when {{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ {{\i d}\\s\\do5({\fs16 {\i p}})}}}{\fldrslt }}} {{<}} 150 micron (corresponding to 100 mesh), internal mass transfer effect was negligible. {\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BMF_transfer \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{?}}}(b) was obtained by varying feed flow rates, while keeping other operating, including contact time (W/F), conditions unchanged. It indicates that when the feed flow rate {{>}} 0.4 mL/min, no significant external mass transfer effect was found. Thus, ({{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ {{\i d}\\s\\do5({\fs16 {\i p}})}}}{\fldrslt }}}) < 150 micron and feed flow rate larger than 0.4 mL/min were used in kinetic measurements. Further, the criteria, as described by Weisz and Prater 
[{\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BIB_Weisz1954 \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{Weisz1954}}}
] and Mears 
[{\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BIB_Mears1971 \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{Mears1971}}}
], were both satisfied as well, confirming the absence of mass and heat transfer effects in all kinetic measurement experiments.\par
{\pard\qj\sl240\slmult1 \sb240 \fi40  \par
\pard\qc\sl240\slmult1 \fi0  \par
\pard\qc\sl240\slmult1 \fi0 {Figure {\*\bkmkstart BMF_transfer}2{\*\bkmkend BMF_transfer}: Mass transfer limitation tests for a) internal diffusion effect and b) external diffusion effect}{\field{\*\fldinst TC "2 Mass transfer limitation tests for a) internal diffusion effect and b) external diffusion effect" \\f f}{\fldrslt }}\par
}\pard\qj\sl240\slmult1 \sb360 \fi0 {\s4\ql\sb240\sa60\keepn\f13\b\fs32 4.3  Kinetic model development\par
}\pard\qj\sl240\slmult1 \sb60 \fi0 In the reaction pathways shown in {\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BMF_network \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{?}}}, each step (R1-R4) contains more than one elementary reaction. Step R1, converting glycerol to arolein, involves two sequential dehydration reactions 
[{\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BIB_Gu2012 \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{Gu2012}}}, {\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BIB_Zhang2015 \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{Zhang2015}}}, {\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BIB_Galadima2016b \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{Galadima2016b}}}
], while more complex reactions (e.g. hydrogenation, dehydrogenation, deoxygenation, etc.) exist when other oxygenates are produced 
[{\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BIB_Park2015 \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{Park2015}}}, {\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BIB_Tamiyakul2015 \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{Tamiyakul2015}}}, {\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BIB_Wang2016 \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{Wang2016}}}
]. Step R2 covers deoxygenation, dehydrogenation and C-C bond cleavage reactions, while step R4 includes oligmerization, cyclization and dehydrogenation. Step 3 refers to pathways where C{{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ {\\s\\do5({\fs16 1})}}}{\fldrslt }}}-C{{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ {\\s\\do5({\fs16 3})}}}{\fldrslt }}} gases do not exist. Thus the overall GTH conversion covers a complex network, which can be reduced to a few equivalent lumped reactions: steps R1-R4. As lumped pseudo-components, oxygenates represent a mixture of acetaldehyde, propanal, acrolein and hydroxyacetone (acetol). Gases represent a mixture of methane, ethylene and propylene, while aromatics refer to C{{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ {\\s\\do5({\fs16 6})}}}{\fldrslt }}}-C{{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ {\\s\\do5({\fs16 12})}}}{\fldrslt }}} aromatic hydrocarbons.\par
\pard\qj\sl240\slmult1 \fi40 The reaction rates of step Ri and their turnover frequencies (TOF) are defined by Eq. ({\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BMEq1 \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{?}}}) and Eq. ({\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BMEq2 \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{?}}}), respectively\par
{\par\par
\pard\tqc\tx3450\tqr\tx6900\tab {\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ {{\i r}\\s\\do5({\fs16 {\i i}})= \\F({\i d}{\i F}\\s\\do5({\fs16 {\i i}}),{\i d}{\i W})}}}{\fldrslt }}\tab{\b0 ({\*\bkmkstart BMEq1}1{\*\bkmkend BMEq1})}\par
}{\par\par
\pard\tqc\tx3450\tqr\tx6900\tab {\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ {{\i T}{\i O}{\i F}\\s\\do5({\fs16 {\i i}})= \\F({\i d}{\i F}\\s\\do5({\fs16 {\i i}}),{\i d} \\b ({\i a}{\i c}{\i t}{\i i}{\i v}{\i e}\~{\i s}{\i i}{\i t}{\i e}))}}}{\fldrslt }}\tab{\b0 ({\*\bkmkstart BMEq2}2{\*\bkmkend BMEq2})}\par
}\pard\qj\sl240\slmult1 \fi40 Based on our prior work and GTH product distribution over various catalysts, surface Pd and acidic sites of H-ZSM-5 are proposed to be active sites. Since only one catalyst (Pd/H-ZSM-5), with specific Pd metal loading amount (wt%=5%) and specific metal dispersion (D%=29.7%), was used in kinetics measurements, the TOF and reaction rate for a specific step (R1-R4) are inter-converted. Because all kinetics measurements were conduced differential operating conditions, corresponding to conversion = 5-10 %. The rate of a differential reaction is thus calculated by Eq. ({\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BMEq3 \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{?}}})\par
{\par\par
\pard\tqc\tx3450\tqr\tx6900\tab {\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ {{\i r}\\s\\do5({\fs16 {\i i}})={\i F}\\s\\do5({\fs16 0}){\f6\u-3916\'B4} \\F({\i X}\\s\\do5({\fs16 {\i i}}),{\i W})}}}{\fldrslt }}\tab{\b0 ({\*\bkmkstart BMEq3}3{\*\bkmkend BMEq3})}\par
}\pard\qj\sl240\slmult1 \fi40 The consumption/formation rates of glycerol and lumped components (oxygenates, gases and aromatics) are shown by Eqs. ({\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BMEq4 \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{?}}} -\~{\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BMEq7 \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{?}}}).\par
{\par\par
