}{\par\par
\pard\tqc\tx3450\tqr\tx6900\tab {\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ { \\F({\i d}{\i F}\\s\\do5({\fs16 {\i O}{\i X}{\i Y}}),{\i d}{\i W})={\i r}\\s\\do5({\fs16 1}){\f6\u-4051\'2D}{\i r}\\s\\do5({\fs16 2}){\f6\u-4051\'2D}{\i r}\\s\\do5({\fs16 3})}}}{\fldrslt }}\tab{\b0 ({\*\bkmkstart BMEq5}5{\*\bkmkend BMEq5})}\par
}{\par\par
\pard\tqc\tx3450\tqr\tx6900\tab {\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ { \\F({\i d}{\i F}\\s\\do5({\fs16 {\i G}{\i A}{\i S}}),{\i d}{\i W})={\i r}\\s\\do5({\fs16 2}){\f6\u-4051\'2D}{\i r}\\s\\do5({\fs16 4})}}}{\fldrslt }}\tab{\b0 ({\*\bkmkstart BMEq6}6{\*\bkmkend BMEq6})}\par
}{\par\par
\pard\tqc\tx3450\tqr\tx6900\tab {\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ { \\F({\i d}{\i F}\\s\\do5({\fs16 {\i A}{\i R}{\i O}}),{\i d}{\i W})={\i r}\\s\\do5({\fs16 3})+{\i r}\\s\\do5({\fs16 4})}}}{\fldrslt }}\tab{\b0 ({\*\bkmkstart BMEq7}7{\*\bkmkend BMEq7})}\par
}\pard\qj\sl240\slmult1 \fi40 Note that among the four equations Eqs. ({\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BMEq4 \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{?}}} -\~{\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BMEq7 \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{?}}}), only three of them are independent. An overall material balance yields the following Eqs. (\~{\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BMEq8 \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{?}}} -\~{\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BMEq10 \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{?}}}).\par
{\par\par
\pard\tqc\tx3450\tqr\tx6900\tab {\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ {{\i F}\\s\\do5({\fs16 {\i G}{\i L}{\i Y}})+{\i F}\\s\\do5({\fs16 {\i O}{\i X}{\i Y}})+{\i F}\\s\\do5({\fs16 {\i G}{\i A}{\i S}})+{\i F}\\s\\do5({\fs16 {\i A}{\i R}{\i O}})={\i F}\\s\\do5({\fs16 {\i i}{\i n}{\i i}})}}}{\fldrslt }}\tab{\b0 ({\*\bkmkstart BMEq8}8{\*\bkmkend BMEq8})}\par
}{\par\par
\pard\tqc\tx3450\tqr\tx6900\tab {\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ {{\i P}\\s\\do5({\fs16 {\i i}})={\i F}\\s\\do5({\fs16 {\i i}}){\f6\u-3916\'B4} \\F({\i P}\\s\\do5({\fs16 0}),{\i F}\\s\\do5({\fs16 0}))}}}{\fldrslt }}\tab{\b0 ({\*\bkmkstart BMEq10}9{\*\bkmkend BMEq10})}\par
}\pard\qj\sl240\slmult1 \sb120 \fi0 {\s4\ql\sb240\sa60\keepn\f13\b\fs32 4.4  Glycerol to Oxygenates (Step R1)\par
}\pard\qj\sl240\slmult1 \sb60 \fi0 Since excess H{{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ {\\s\\do5({\fs16 2})}}}{\fldrslt }}} (molar ratio of H{{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ {\\s\\do5({\fs16 2})}}}{\fldrslt }}} to glycerol is {{{\f6\'7E}}}10:1) is used in all cases, H{{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ {\\s\\do5({\fs16 2})}}}{\fldrslt }}} pressure effect was considered as a constant contribution to the reaction rates. Kinetics measurements were carried out at various temperatures in the range of 350-450 {{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ {\\s\\up5({\fs16 {\f6\u-3985\'6F}})}}}{\fldrslt }}}C. Although only results at 400 {{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ {\\s\\up5({\fs16 {\f6\u-3985\'6F}})}}}{\fldrslt }}} C, as the standard temperature, are shown below, which gives optimum aromatic yields 
[{\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BIB_Xiao2016 \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{Xiao2016}}}
], fitting results for other temperatures presented essentially the same conclusions. All experiments were repeated at least twice, while temperature effects are discussed in later section.\par
\pard\qj\sl240\slmult1 \fi40 When glycerol was fed, under differential operating conditions (glycerol conversion typically {{<}} 5-10%), all lumped components (oxygenates, gases and aromatics) were detected. By varying glycerol feeding flow rates, the consuming rates of glycerol, with respect to average bed reactant partial pressures, were measured, as shown and fitted in {\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BMF_glycerol \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{?}}}.\par
{\pard\qj\sl240\slmult1 \sb240 \fi40  \par
\pard\qc\sl240\slmult1 \fi0  \par
\pard\qc\sl240\slmult1 \fi0 {Figure {\*\bkmkstart BMF_glycerol}3{\*\bkmkend BMF_glycerol}: Fit of Glycerol Initial Reaction Rates}{\field{\*\fldinst TC "3 Fit of Glycerol Initial Reaction Rates" \\f f}{\fldrslt }}\par
}\pard\qj\sl240\slmult1 \sb240 \fi0 As described by Eq. ({\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BMEq4 \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{?}}}), the consuming rates of glycerol are only dependent on glycerol partial pressure. Since a linear fit works well for glycerol initial reaction rates, step R1 appears to be a first-order reaction, which is consistent with literature 
[{\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BIB_Akizuki2012 \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{Akizuki2012}}}, {\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BIB_Talebian_Kiakalaieh2014 \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{Talebian\\s\\do5({\fs16 K})iakalaieh2014}}}, {\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BIB_Park2015 \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{Park2015}}}, {\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BIB_Talebian_Kiakalaieh2015 \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{Talebian\\s\\do5({\fs16 K})iakalaieh2015}}}
] Thus, Eq. ({\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BMEq4 \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{?}}}) can be re-written as Eq. ({\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BMEq11 \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{?}}}).\par
{\par\par
\pard\tqc\tx3450\tqr\tx6900\tab {\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ {{\f6\u-4051\'2D} \\F({\i d}{\i F}\\s\\do5({\fs16 {\i G}{\i L}{\i Y}}),{\i d}\\s\\do5({\fs16 {\i W}}))={\i r}\\s\\do5({\fs16 1})={\i k}\\s\\do5({\fs16 1}){\i P}\\s\\do5({\fs16 {\i G}{\i L}{\i Y}})}}}{\fldrslt }}\tab{\b0 ({\*\bkmkstart BMEq11}10{\*\bkmkend BMEq11})}\par
}\pard\qj\sl240\slmult1 \sb120 \fi0 {\s4\ql\sb240\sa60\keepn\f13\b\fs32 4.5  Oxygenates as Reactant (Steps R2 and R3)\par
}\pard\qj\sl240\slmult1 \sb60 \fi0 In the above section, the rate constant and reaction order of step R1 were obtained by feeding glycerol in a differential reactor, controlling glycerol conversions were under 5-10 %. To study the rate constants and reaction orders of steps R2-R4, still under differential operating conditions, oxygenates were used as feed. The oxygenates, based on the compositions reported in our prior work 
[{\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BIB_Xiao2016 \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{Xiao2016}}}
], were represented by a mixture of acetaldehyde, propanal, acrolein and acetol. Because acrolein was found to be the primary component (60-93%), pure acrolein was also tested as a representative component. As shown in {\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BMF_oxygenates \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{?}}}, both oxygenates and pure acrolein curves show good secondary-order fits, while generating rates of gases and aromatics, corresponding to {{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ {{\i r}\\s\\do5({\fs16 2})}}}{\fldrslt }}}-{{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ {{\i r}\\s\\do5({\fs16 4})}}}{\fldrslt }}} and {{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ {{\i r}\\s\\do5({\fs16 3})}}}{\fldrslt }}}+{{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ {{\i r}\\s\\do5({\fs16 4})}}}{\fldrslt }}}, appears to be secondary orders as well.\par
\pard\qj\sl240\slmult1 \fi40 The conversion of acrolein to gases (olefins) likely includes hydrogenation of arolein to propanol, followed by propanol dehydration and other C-C cleavage reactions. 
[{\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BIB_Marinelli1995 \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{Marinelli1995}}}, {\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BIB_Mohr2000 \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{Mohr2000}}}, {\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BIB_Volckmar2009 \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{Volckmar2009}}}
]. Both first and second order were reported for acrolein hydrogenation. 
[{\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BIB_Jesus1999 \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{Jesus1999}}}, {\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BIB_Wei2013 \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{Wei2013}}}
] Since reaction rates starting with representative oxygenates and pure acrolein are close, as shown in {\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BMF_oxygenates \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{?}}}, only pure arolein was used for further kinetic measurements, discussion and analysis.\par
{\pard\qj\sl240\slmult1 \sb240 \fi40  \par
\pard\qc\sl240\slmult1 \fi0  \par
\pard\qc\sl240\slmult1 \fi0 {Figure {\*\bkmkstart BMF_oxygenates}4{\*\bkmkend BMF_oxygenates}: Fit of Oxygenate Initial Reaction Rates}{\field{\*\fldinst TC "4 Fit of Oxygenate Initial Reaction Rates" \\f f}{\fldrslt }}\par
}\pard\qj\sl240\slmult1 \sb240 \fi0 Both gases and aromatics were detected when oxygenates conversions were lower than 10%. Since no glycerol was fed ({{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ {{\i r}\\s\\do5({\fs16 1})}}}{\fldrslt }}}=0), the consuming rate of oxygenates is dependent only on {{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ {{\i r}\\s\\do5({\fs16 2})}}}{\fldrslt }}} and {{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ {{\i r}\\s\\do5({\fs16 3})}}}{\fldrslt }}}, as described by Eq. ({\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BMEq5 \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{?}}}). {\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BMF_oxygenates \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{?}}} shows that the overall reaction rates of {{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ {{\i r}\\s\\do5({\fs16 2})}}}{\fldrslt }}} + {{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ {{\i r}\\s\\do5({\fs16 3})}}}{\fldrslt }}} follow a secondary order. It is assumed here that reaction orders of steps R2 and R3 are the same, thus {{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ {{\i n}\\s\\do5({\fs16 2})}}}{\fldrslt }}}={{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ {{\i n}\\s\\do5({\fs16 3})}}}{\fldrslt }}}=2. Eq. ({\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BMEq5 \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{?}}} -\~{\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BMEq7 \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{?}}}) therefore are re-written as Eq. ({\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BMEq12 \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{?}}} -\~{\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BMEq15 \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{?}}}).\par
{\par\par
\pard\tqc\tx3450\tqr\tx6900\tab {\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ {{\f6\u-4051\'2D} \\F({\i d}{\i F}\\s\\do5({\fs16 {\i O}{\i X}{\i Y}}),{\i d}{\i W})={\i r}\\s\\do5({\fs16 2})+{\i r}\\s\\do5({\fs16 3})={\i k}\\s\\do5({\fs16 2}){\i P}\\s\\up({\fs16 2,{\i O}{\i X}{\i Y}})+{\i k}\\s\\do5({\fs16 3}){\i P}\\s\\up({\fs16 2,{\i O}{\i X}{\i Y}})}}}{\fldrslt }}\tab{\b0 ({\*\bkmkstart BMEq12}11{\*\bkmkend BMEq12})}\par
}{\par\par
\pard\tqc\tx3450\tqr\tx6900\tab {\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ { \\F({\i d}{\i F}\\s\\do5({\fs16 {\i G}{\i A}{\i S}}),{\i d}{\i W})={\i r}\\s\\do5({\fs16 2}){\f6\u-4051\'2D}{\i r}\\s\\do5({\fs16 4})={\i k}\\s\\do5({\fs16 2}){\i P}\\s\\up({\fs16 2,{\i O}{\i X}{\i Y}}){\f6\u-4051\'2D}{\i k}\\s\\do5({\fs16 4}){\i P}\\s\\up({\fs16 {\i n}\\s\\do4({\fs13 4}),{\i G}{\i A}{\i S}})}}}{\fldrslt }}\tab{\b0 ({\*\bkmkstart BMEq14}12{\*\bkmkend BMEq14})}\par
}{\par\par
\pard\tqc\tx3450\tqr\tx6900\tab {\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ { \\F({\i d}{\i F}\\s\\do5({\fs16 {\i A}{\i R}{\i O}}),{\i d}{\i W})={\i r}\\s\\do5({\fs16 3})+{\i r}\\s\\do5({\fs16 4})={\i k}\\s\\do5({\fs16 3}){\i P}\\s\\up({\fs16 2,{\i O}{\i X}{\i Y}})+{\i k}\\s\\do5({\fs16 4}){\i P}\\s\\up({\fs16 {\i n}\\s\\do4({\fs13 4}),{\i G}{\i A}{\i S}})}}}{\fldrslt }}\tab{\b0 ({\*\bkmkstart BMEq15}13{\*\bkmkend BMEq15})}\par
}\pard\qj\sl240\slmult1 \fi40 To determine reaction order of step R4 ({{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ {{\i n}\\s\\do5({\fs16 4})}}}{\fldrslt }}}) and all rate constants ({{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ {{\i k}\\s\\do5({\fs16 2})}}}{\fldrslt }}}-{{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ {{\i k}\\s\\do5({\fs16 4})}}}{\fldrslt }}}), Eq. ({\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BMEq16 \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{?}}}) is obtained by Eq. ({\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BMEq15 \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{?}}}) / Eq. ({\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BMEq14 \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{?}}}):\par
{\par\par
\pard\tqc\tx3450\tqr\tx6900\tab {\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ { \\F({\i d}{\i F}\\s\\do5({\fs16 {\i A}{\i R}{\i O}}),{\i d}{\i F}\\s\\do5({\fs16 {\i G}{\i A}{\i S}}))= \\F({\i k}\\s\\do5({\fs16 3}){\i P}\\s\\up({\fs16 2,{\i O}{\i X}{\i Y}})+{\i k}\\s\\do5({\fs16 4}){\i P}\\s\\up({\fs16 {\i n}\\s\\do4({\fs13 4}),{\i G}{\i A}{\i S}}),{\i k}\\s\\do5({\fs16 2}){\i P}\\s\\up({\fs16 2,{\i O}{\i X}{\i Y}}){\f6\u-4051\'2D}{\i k}\\s\\do5({\fs16 4}){\i P}\\s\\up({\fs16 {\i n}\\s\\do4({\fs13 4}),{\i G}{\i A}{\i S}}))={\f6\u-3999\'61}}}}{\fldrslt }}\tab{\b0 ({\*\bkmkstart BMEq16}14{\*\bkmkend BMEq16})}\par
}\pard\qj\sl240\slmult1 \fi40 With respect to partial pressure of gases, the aromatics partial pressure is plotted in {\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BMF_234 \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{?}}}(a). Since a linear correlation fits well, as shown in {\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BMF_234 \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{?}}}(a), for {{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ {{\i P}\\s\\do5({\fs16 {\i A}{\i R}{\i O}})}}}{\fldrslt }}} versus {{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ {{\i P}\\s\\do5({\fs16 {\i G}{\i A}{\i S}})}}}{\fldrslt }}}, Eq. ({\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BMEq16 \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{?}}}) equals a constant, which is assigned as {{{\f6\'61}}}.\par
\pard\qj\sl240\slmult1 \fi40 Thus, the following Eq. ({\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BMEq17 \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{?}}}) also equals a constant, which is assigned as {{{\f6\'62}}}.\par
{\par\par
\pard\tqc\tx3450\tqr\tx6900\tab {\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ { \\F({\i P}\\s\\up({\fs16 {\i n}\\s\\do4({\fs13 4}),{\i G}{\i A}{\i S}}),{\i P}\\s\\up({\fs16 2,{\i O}{\i X}{\i Y}}))= \\F({\f6\u-3999\'61}{\i k}\\s\\do5({\fs16 2}){\f6\u-4051\'2D}{\i k}\\s\\do5({\fs16 3}),{\i k}\\s\\do5({\fs16 4})({\f6\u-3999\'61}+1))={\f6\u-3998\'62}}}}{\fldrslt }}\tab{\b0 ({\*\bkmkstart BMEq17}15{\*\bkmkend BMEq17})}\par
}\pard\qj\sl240\slmult1 \fi40 Thus, Eq. ({\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BMEq18 \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{?}}}) is obtained by the use of logarithm forms.\par
{\par\par
\pard\tqc\tx3450\tqr\tx6900\tab {\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ {{\i l}{\i o}{\i g}{\i P}\\s\\up({\fs16 2,{\i O}{\i X}{\i Y}})={\i n}\\s\\do5({\fs16 4}){\i l}{\i o}{\i g}{\i P}\\s\\do5({\fs16 {\i G}{\i A}{\i S}}){\f6\u-4051\'2D}{\i l}{\i o}{\i g}{\f6\u-3998\'62}}}}{\fldrslt }}\tab{\b0 ({\*\bkmkstart BMEq18}16{\*\bkmkend BMEq18})}\par
}\pard\qj\sl240\slmult1 \fi40 The correlation of {{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ {{\i P}\\s\\up({\fs16 {\i n}\\s\\do4({\fs13 4}),{\i G}{\i A}{\i S}})}}}{\fldrslt }}} and {{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ {{\i P}\\s\\up({\fs16 2,{\i O}{\i X}{\i Y}})}}}{\fldrslt }}} are plotted in {\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BMF_234 \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{?}}} (b), where the fitted slope appears to be 0.968. Thus step 4 is found to be a first-order reaction.\par
\pard\qj\sl240\slmult1 \fi40 More tests for reaction orders of steps R2 and R3 were carried out, such as the following data sets: {{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ {{\i n}\\s\\do5({\fs16 2})}}}{\fldrslt }}}=1.0, {{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ {{\i n}\\s\\do5({\fs16 3})}}}{\fldrslt }}}=1.0; {{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ {{\i n}\\s\\do5({\fs16 2})}}}{\fldrslt }}}=1.0, {{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ {{\i n}\\s\\do5({\fs16 3})}}}{\fldrslt }}}=2.0; {{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ {{\i n}\\s\\do5({\fs16 2})}}}{\fldrslt }}}=2.0, {{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ {{\i n}\\s\\do5({\fs16 3})}}}{\fldrslt }}}=1.0;{{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ {{\i n}\\s\\do5({\fs16 2})}}}{\fldrslt }}}=2.0, {{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ {{\i n}\\s\\do5({\fs16 3})}}}{\fldrslt }}}=3.0; and {{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ {{\i n}\\s\\do5({\fs16 2})}}}{\fldrslt }}}=3.0, {{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ {{\i n}\\s\\do5({\fs16 3})}}}{\fldrslt }}}=2.0, etc. Data fit under these sets always provide worse fits. Thus it is confirmed that the assumption of {{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ {{\i n}\\s\\do5({\fs16 2})}}}{\fldrslt }}}=2.0, {{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ {{\i n}\\s\\do5({\fs16 3})}}}{\fldrslt }}}=2.0 is reasonable.\par
{\pard\qj\sl240\slmult1 \sb240 \fi40  \par
\pard\qc\sl240\slmult1 \fi0  \par
\pard\qc\sl240\slmult1 \fi0 {Figure {\*\bkmkstart BMF_234}5{\*\bkmkend BMF_234}: Fit of Steps R2, R3 and R4}{\field{\*\fldinst TC "5 Fit of Steps R2, R3 and R4" \\f f}{\fldrslt }}\par
}\pard\qj\sl240\slmult1 \sb360 \fi0 {\s4\ql\sb240\sa60\keepn\f13\b\fs32 4.6  Temperature Effect\par
}\pard\qj\sl240\slmult1 \sb60 \fi0 All data discussed in above sections was measured at 400 {{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ {\\s\\up5({\fs16 {\f6\u-3985\'6F}})}}}{\fldrslt }}}C, while the reaction orders ({{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ {{\i n}\\s\\do5({\fs16 1})}}}{\fldrslt }}}-{{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ {{\i n}\\s\\do5({\fs16 4})}}}{\fldrslt }}}) remain the same when other temperatures (300-450 {{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ {\\s\\up5({\fs16 {\f6\u-3985\'6F}})}}}{\fldrslt }}}C) are used. Reaction activation energies ({{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ {{\i E}\\s\\do5({\fs16 {\i a}})}}}{\fldrslt }}}) of steps (R1-R4) are calculated using the Arrhenius equation ({\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BMEq31 \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{?}}}).\par
{\par\par
