}\pard\qj\sl240\slmult1 \sb240 \fi0 The activation energy of step R1 (glycerol dehydration to acrolein) has been reported in a wide range in literature. Talebian-Kiakalaieh et al. presented a value of 27.5 kJ/mol over tungstosilicic acid catalyst 
[{\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BIB_Talebian_Kiakalaieh2015 \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{Talebian\\s\\do5({\fs16 K})iakalaieh2015}}}
], Qadariyah et al. presented a value of 39.6 kJ/mol in sub-critical or supercritical water without any catalyst. 
[{\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BIB_Qadariyah2011 \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{Qadariyah2011}}}
] Watanabe et al. gave a value of 146 kJ/mol at 300-400 {{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ {\\s\\up5({\fs16 {\f6\u-3985\'6F}})}}}{\fldrslt }}}C with H{{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ {\\s\\do5({\fs16 2})}}}{\fldrslt }}}SO{{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ {\\s\\do5({\fs16 4})}}}{\fldrslt }}} catalyst 
[{\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BIB_Watanabe2007 \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{Watanabe2007}}}
], while Ott et al. report a value of 140 kJ/mol at 300-360 {{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ {\\s\\up5({\fs16 {\f6\u-3985\'6F}})}}}{\fldrslt }}}C over zinc sulfate catalyst 
[{\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BIB_Ott2006 \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{Ott2006}}}
]. Our result described in {\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BMT_Ea \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{?}}} is in the reported range of 27.5-146 kJ/mol.\par
\pard\qj\sl240\slmult1 \fi40 The activation energy of step R2 is smaller than that of step R3 by 26 kJ/mol, indicating step R2 dominates in the reaction pathways, as compared to step R3. As a series reaction, step R4, with a slightly higher activation energy, is followed by step R2.\par
\pard\qj\sl240\slmult1 \fi40 All the present works were carried out under differential operating conditions, while integral conditions were applied in our prior work. 
[{\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BIB_Xiao2016 \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{Xiao2016}}}
] Now all kinetic parameters, including rate constants, reaction orders, activation energies and exponential factors, for steps R1-R4 are known. All the 80 data points for experimental and predicted flow rates are plotted in {\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BMF_parity \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{?}}}, giving an RMS error of 6.1%, indicating good fits.\par
{\pard\qj\sl240\slmult1 \sb240 \fi40  \par
\pard\qc\sl240\slmult1 \fi0  \par
\pard\qc\sl240\slmult1 \fi0 {Figure {\*\bkmkstart BMF_parity}6{\*\bkmkend BMF_parity}: A parity plot for comparison of experimental and predicted flow rates}{\field{\*\fldinst TC "6 A parity plot for comparison of experimental and predicted flow rates" \\f f}{\fldrslt }}\par
}\pard\qj\sl240\slmult1 \sb480 \fi0 {\s3\ql\sb240\sa60\keepn\f13\b\fs32 5  Concluding Remarks\par
}\pard\qj\sl240\slmult1 \sb60 \fi0 In the present work, differential kinetic experiments were carried out for glycerol to hydrocarbon fuels conversion. A reaction pathway and kinetic model were developed based on experimental observation and modeling. Reaction orders and activation energies for each step in the proposed pathways were calculated. Thee model predicted data match well with experimental results. This work provides a practical model for converting glycerol, as biodiesel by-product, to hydrocarbon fuels.\par
{\pard\qj\sl240\slmult1 \fi0 \par
